 "checked_text": "this page is a non-preemptive sjf schedule: gray is waiting, black is running. we'll answer your question by following each cpu-free moment, not by eyeballing the bars.\n\nat time 0, p1, p2, and p3 are all ready with burst 1. the tie goes by listing order, so p1 runs first, then p2, then p3.\np1 wait 0\np2 wait 1\np3 wait 2\n\nat time 3, the ready bursts are p4=1, p5=2, and p6=3. sjf picks them in increasing burst length: p4, then p5, then p6.\np4 wait 0\np5 wait 1\np6 wait 3\n\nhere's the key part: p7, p8, and p9 all arrive at 7, but the cpu is busy with p6 until 9. at 9, their bursts are 3, 2, and 1, so sjf runs p9, then p8, then p7.\ncpu frees\np9 wait 2\np8 wait 3\n\nafter p9 and p8 finish, only p7 remains from the time-7 group. it finally runs 12 to 15, so its waiting time is 12 minus 7, which is 5.\np7 wait 5\n12-7=5\n\nnow look at p12: it arrives at 13, but at each choice point a shorter ready job exists. p10 and p11 go first, then new arrivals p13 and p14 are shorter than p12.\np10 wait 2\np11 wait 3\np13 wait 1\n\nat time 19, p14 is still shorter than p12, so p14 runs before it. only at time 21 is p12 the only ready process, so p12 waits 21 minus 13: 8 units.\np14 wait 2\np12 wait 8\n21-13=8\n\nso the answer is: p9, p8, p7 run by burst lengths 1, 2, 3; p12 waits because shorter jobs keep appearing. adding all waits gives 33, and 33 divided by 14 is 2.36.\n33/14 = 2.36\ncpu free -> find ready jobs\nfind ready jobs -> pick shortest burst\npick shortest burst -> run to finish\nrun to finish -> record wait\nrecord wait -> cpu free"
